import{c as t,Q as a,j as o,m as n}from"./chunks/framework.BXc4yaFE.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Subagents and tokenomics","description":"","frontmatter":{"url":"https://x.com/davidvgilmore/status/2064783898001743948","title":"Subagents and tokenomics","author":"David Valerio Gilmore","archived":"2026-06-11T00:00:00.000Z","handle":"@davidvgilmore","published":"2026-06-10T00:00:00.000Z","stats":"16 likes · 5 retweets · 32 bookmarks · 3750 views","tags":["subagents","tokenomics","model-routing","agent-architecture","cache","frontier-model","trailing-edge"],"type":"twitter-article"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"research/tweets/2026-06-10-@davidvgilmore-743948.md","filePath":"research/tweets/2026-06-10-@davidvgilmore-743948.md","lastUpdated":1785909234000}'),r={name:"research/tweets/2026-06-10-@davidvgilmore-743948.md"};function s(i,e,l,h,d,c){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="subagents-and-tokenomics-—-davidvgilmore" tabindex="-1">Subagents and tokenomics — @davidvgilmore <a class="header-anchor" href="#subagents-and-tokenomics-—-davidvgilmore" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Subagents and tokenomics — @davidvgilmore&quot;">​</a></h1><p><strong>來源</strong>：<a href="https://x.com/davidvgilmore/status/2064783898001743948" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://x.com/davidvgilmore/status/2064783898001743948</a><strong>作者</strong>：David Valerio Gilmore（@davidvgilmore） <strong>發布日期</strong>：2026-06-10 <strong>收錄日期</strong>：2026-06-11 <strong>類型</strong>：Twitter Article <strong>統計</strong>：16 likes · 5 retweets · 32 bookmarks · 3750 views</p><hr><h2 id="全文" tabindex="-1">全文 <a class="header-anchor" href="#全文" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;全文&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The most notable thing about the Claude Fable 5 release is not the model. It&#39;s that Claude Code shipped nested subagents, capped at depth 5, at the same time. Max depth 5 sounds like an implementation detail, but it&#39;s not. In human organizations, five real levels of hierarchy usually do not show up until a company has hundreds to low-thousands, of employees. You don&#39;t add that much structure because hierarchy is elegant. You add it because there is too much work, too much context, and too many parallel decisions for one person, or one layer, to manage directly. That is now happening inside coding agents. The old loop was simple: a user asks for something, the agent reads files, edits files, runs tests, and reports back. The new loop is starting to look more like an organization: a lead agent plans the work, research agents inspect the repo, implementation agents make bounded changes, test agents verify behavior, critic agents challenge the result, and some of those agents may launch their own agents. @emollick&#39;s write-up on Claude Fable 5 is interesting for exactly this reason. The demos are impressive, but the deeper point is that Fable behaves like a manager. In the isochrone-map example, it delegated research to other agents, kept coding while they ran, then launched more agents to check and attack the result. The frontier model is becoming less like the only worker in the system and more like the manager of a temporary software organization. That changes the cost picture. If the smartest model is also the best manager, you probably do want it in the loop. But you do not want it doing every token of labor. You want it making the high-judgement calls, then routing narrower work to cheaper, faster, more specialized workers where possible. This is more than a commentary on an interesting agent architectural pattern. It gets at the most economically important question of today: how might we make frontier-quality agents abundant, when frontier-quality models are so expensive? I&#39;ll tackle that in two parts.</p><h1 id="part-1-why-subagents-have-become-a-standard-primitive" tabindex="-1">Part 1: Why subagents have become a standard primitive <a class="header-anchor" href="#part-1-why-subagents-have-become-a-standard-primitive" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Part 1: Why subagents have become a standard primitive&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A few months ago, subagents were a niche power-user primitive. But now the coding agent ecosystem has standardized on them. Every major coding agent harness supports subagents. This happened quickly because four trends converged.</p><h2 id="trend-1-context-windows" tabindex="-1">Trend 1: Context windows <a class="header-anchor" href="#trend-1-context-windows" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Trend 1: Context windows&quot;">​</a></h2><p>From 2023 to 2025, context windows grew at an exponential pace, going from 8k to 1M+ in just a couple years. But that growth in context length for commercial models hasn&#39;t kept pace from 2025 to 2026. [圖 1 — 來源: Context windows grew exponentially from 2023 to 2025.] If the trend held, we&#39;d expect to see more commercial models with 10M+ context windows today. Why haven&#39;t we? I like to think of this in terms of a physical office. While a bigger desk helps keep your work organized, at a certain point the problem is not desk size. The problem is that one person should not be doing every job at the same desk. Subagents solved this and alleviated the pressing need for ever larger context windows. Subagents offer context isolation. A worker can go read forty files, run tests, inspect logs, and return just the three facts the parent model actually needs. The main model preserves intent. The worker absorbs the mess.</p><h2 id="trend-2-model-diversity-increased-at-the-trailing-edge" tabindex="-1">Trend 2: Model diversity increased at the trailing edge <a class="header-anchor" href="#trend-2-model-diversity-increased-at-the-trailing-edge" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Trend 2: Model diversity increased at the trailing edge&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Semiconductors are a useful analogy here. There is a leading edge, where the most advanced chips get built on the newest process nodes. But there is also a huge trailing edge: older, cheaper, more available chips that are not &quot;best&quot; in the abstract, but are exactly right for cars, appliances, sensors, industrial systems, and a lot of the real economy. AI models are starting to look similar. The leading edge still matters enormously. You want Fable, Opus, GPT-5.5, or whatever the best frontier model is for the hard managerial work: ambiguous planning, decomposition, judgement, synthesis, and deciding when something is actually done. But behind that frontier, the trailing edge has become much richer: cheap cloud models, open-weight models, local models, fast models, long-context models, coding specialists, and weirdly capable small models that are not globally best but are Pareto optimal for specific agentic jobs. [圖 2 — 來源: The number of pareto-optimal models for agentic tasks over time.📷] That is what this AgentBench chart shows. The number of Pareto-optimal models for agentic tasks rose from roughly 5 last summer to 23 by May, with a clear inflection around December &#39;25. The point is not that the trailing-edge models have caught the frontier on every task. They haven&#39;t. The point is that there are now many more models that are &quot;best&quot; for some combination of cost, latency, quality, and task type. In a chat product, that diversity is interesting. In a subagent architecture, it becomes economically actionable. A main-thread planning call needs the leading edge. A repo search, summary, dependency fix, documentation pass, or narrow code edit should almost always go to a &quot;trailing edge&quot; model</p><h2 id="trend-3-frontier-models-have-better-long-range-focus" tabindex="-1">Trend 3: Frontier models have better long-range focus <a class="header-anchor" href="#trend-3-frontier-models-have-better-long-range-focus" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Trend 3: Frontier models have better long-range focus&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The frontier is increasingly about time horizon, not just answer quality. Models are getting better at holding a goal over longer runs, launching parallel investigations, recovering from mistakes, and deciding when to go deeper. The model&#39;s highest-value role starts to change: less direct labor, more deciding what labor should exist. [圖 3 — 來源: As AI agents can sustain longer autonomous tasks, delegation becomes more valuable. Long-horizon capability turns the frontier model into a manager, not just a worker.] What needs to be researched? What can be parallelized? What should be delegated? What needs a critic? What is routine? What requires the best model? What should be killed because it is wasting time? That is managerial intelligence. Once it exists, it becomes economically irrational to make the most expensive model do every small job itself.</p><h2 id="trend-4-harnesses-got-better" tabindex="-1">Trend 4: Harnesses got better <a class="header-anchor" href="#trend-4-harnesses-got-better" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Trend 4: Harnesses got better&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A coding agent is not just a model. It is a model inside a harness: tools, permissions, repo access, shell access, memory, worktrees, tests, hooks, background tasks, and yes, subagents (a recursive invocation of the Agent itself as a tool). That harness changes what smaller models can do. A small model asked to &quot;build the app&quot; will often fail. A small model asked to &quot;inspect these five files and summarize the likely source of this failing test&quot; can be useful. The parent supplies intent. The harness supplies boundaries: worktrees provide isolation, hooks provide deterministic actions. The subagent supplies focused labor. Tests, critics, or the parent model verify the result. That combination makes weaker models much more valuable than they look in standalone benchmarks. [圖 4 — 來源: The broader backdrop is that AI capability is spreading across domains. Once many tasks become feasible, the economic question shifts from &quot;can AI do it?&quot; to &quot;which model should do this part?&quot;]</p><h1 id="part-2-why-subagents-are-the-natural-leverage-point-for-efficiency" tabindex="-1">Part 2: Why subagents are the natural leverage point for efficiency <a class="header-anchor" href="#part-2-why-subagents-are-the-natural-leverage-point-for-efficiency" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Part 2: Why subagents are the natural leverage point for efficiency&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A coding-agent run is not one kind of work. It is planning, repo discovery, file reading, debugging, lint fixes, log inspection, refactoring, documentation, critique, verification, and final synthesis all mixed together. These jobs do not sit on one cost-quality curve. Conceptually, there is a curve for repo search, another for test triage, another for code review, another for UI edits, another for final synthesis. The model that dominates one curve may be wasteful on another. Subagents make those curves actionable. Each subagent call has a bounded job, bounded context, bounded permissions, and a result that can be checked. Without subagents, model routing is blunt: choose a model for the whole chat or the whole run. With subagents, routing gets precise: keep the lead model on the frontier where judgement matters, and route bounded worker tasks to cheaper cloud models, open models, specialized models, or local models where they are good enough.</p><h2 id="the-precious-need-to-preserve-cache" tabindex="-1">The precious need to preserve cache <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-precious-need-to-preserve-cache" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The precious need to preserve cache&quot;">​</a></h2><p>There is also a more technical reason subagents are the natural routing point: cache. In long coding-agent sessions, the main model accumulates valuable state: the user&#39;s goal, repo findings, tool outputs, failed attempts, test results, constraints, and intermediate plans. Once that context is cached, you do not want to throw it away. [圖 5 — 來源: TikTok reported roughly 50% savings in an internal AI QA agent after adding prompt caching. The lesson for agent systems is that architecture matters: where you preserve context can matter as much as which model you choose.] Switching models inside the main loop can bust that cache. Even if the second model is cheaper per token, rebuilding context can erase a lot of the savings. A subagent call is different. It is already a separate context. The parent keeps its expensive cached state; the worker gets a smaller packet of context and a job to do. That makes subagents a cache-efficient place to route.</p><h2 id="what-90-cheaper-frontier-coding-agents-actually-means" tabindex="-1">What 90% cheaper frontier coding agents actually means <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-90-cheaper-frontier-coding-agents-actually-means" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What 90% cheaper frontier coding agents actually means&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A frontier coding agent does not become cheap because the frontier model gets cheap. It becomes cheap because the frontier model is used more like a manager and less like a laborer. The expensive model should hold the goal, make the plan, judge ambiguous tradeoffs, verify important results, and synthesize the final answer. But a growing share of token volume will come from narrower work: searching, reading, summarizing, checking, testing, critiquing, and retrying.</p><blockquote><p>Subagents are not just a nicer way to organize the agent. They are where the tokens will increasingly go. Today, many sessions use zero or a few subagents. Six months from now, I expect serious autonomous coding runs to routinely use 25 to 100. Large overnight runs will use thousands. At that point, the economics are no longer determined only by the sticker price of the lead model. They are determined by the weighted average cost of all the workers underneath it. That is the @RaylineAI bet. Subagent-focused routing is the leverage point for making frontier coding agents 60–90% cheaper without making them feel worse. The user can still feel like Fable, Opus, or another frontier model is in charge, because top-level judgement still comes from the best model. But under the hood, much of the labor is routed to cheaper cloud models, open models, specialized models, or models running on the user&#39;s own machine. [圖 6 — 來源: Using Fable to rename a file is like having Barack Obama manage the till at McDonald&#39;s. Of course he can do it, but he&#39;s vastly overqualified.] We&#39;re still in an era where people see that the frontier model can do the small job and stop there. Of course it can. It can rename the file, parse the tiny log, fix the import, and write the tiny patch. But the value of the frontier model is not that it can do routine labor. The value is that it can manage the organization of routine labor. The future coding agent is not one giant model doing everything. It is a hierarchy of intelligence: a frontier model managing fleets of smaller, cheaper, specialized workers. Subagents make the work decomposable. Better harnesses make weaker models useful. Model diversity makes routing valuable. Cache makes the routing boundary matter. Frontier planning makes the whole system coherent. That is the path to abundant intelligence: not one god model answering everything, but a great manager and a lot of good workers.</p></blockquote><hr><h2 id="研究摘要" tabindex="-1">研究摘要 <a class="header-anchor" href="#研究摘要" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;研究摘要&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>TL;DR</strong>：Subagents 是讓 frontier 模型成本降低 60-90% 的關鍵槓桿——frontier 做管理決策（保持 cache），worker 任務精確路由到 trailing-edge 模型；未來嚴肅 coding run 將用 25-100 個 subagents。</p><h3 id="核心論點" tabindex="-1">核心論點 <a class="header-anchor" href="#核心論點" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;核心論點&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li><strong>Depth 5 subagents = 組織型 hierarchy</strong>：Fable 5 + nested subagents max depth 5 代表 agent 架構從單一 worker loop 演化為 lead/research/implementation/test/critic 五層組織</li><li><strong>四個趨勢讓 subagents 成標準 primitive</strong>：context window 成長停滯（隔離解決問題）/ trailing-edge 模型多樣化（23 Pareto-optimal）/ frontier 長程焦點增強 / harness 更好（bounded task 讓弱模型可用）</li><li><strong>Subagent 是精確模型路由的邊界</strong>：沒有 subagent = 整個 run 用同一個模型；有 subagent = frontier 管理、bounded worker task 路由到 trailing-edge</li><li><strong>Cache 是路由邊界的重要技術理由</strong>：main loop 的 cached context 珍貴；switching models in-loop 破壞 cache；subagent 已是獨立 context，不傷 parent cache</li><li><strong>Tokenomics 轉移</strong>：成本由 lead model 定價轉為所有 workers 的加權平均；6 個月後預計 25-100 subagents/serious run，overnight runs 達數千個</li></ol><h3 id="關鍵數據-工具" tabindex="-1">關鍵數據 / 工具 <a class="header-anchor" href="#關鍵數據-工具" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;關鍵數據 / 工具&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>AgentBench：Pareto-optimal agentic models 從 ~5（2025 夏）→ 23（2026 五月），12 月出現明顯轉折</li><li>TikTok：內部 AI QA agent 加入 prompt caching 後節省 ~50%</li><li>預測：6 個月後嚴肅 coding run 25-100 subagents；overnight run 達數千個</li><li>@RaylineAI：subagent-focused routing → frontier coding agents 60-90% cheaper</li><li>Context window 成長：2023-2025 指數增長（8K→1M+），2025-2026 停滯（10M+ 未出現）</li></ul><h3 id="批判性觀察" tabindex="-1">批判性觀察 <a class="header-anchor" href="#批判性觀察" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;批判性觀察&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li><strong>作者顯然推銷 @RaylineAI</strong>：文章最後段明確提到「That is the @RaylineAI bet」，影響中立性；60-90% 數字缺乏作者自身的實測數據</li><li><strong>TikTok 的 50% 節省被 over-extended</strong>：那是 prompt caching 的效果，非 subagent routing；直接拿來支持 subagent 效率論點有邏輯跳躍</li><li><strong>「Trailing-edge 模型 Pareto-optimal」的 routing 實踐細節缺失</strong>：哪個任務該用哪類模型、如何動態決策、router 本身成本 — 文章停在概念層，未給操作準則</li></ol><hr><h2 id="評分" tabindex="-1">評分 <a class="header-anchor" href="#評分" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;評分&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>維度</th><th>分數</th><th>說明</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A. Workspace 可行動性 (30%)</td><td>7/10</td><td>架構概念清晰（frontier manager + trailing workers），但缺乏具體任務→模型映射表</td></tr><tr><td>B. 創新性 (20%)</td><td>8/10</td><td>Cache as routing boundary + tokenomics 轉移論述（weighted avg cost）是有洞見的新框架</td></tr><tr><td>C. 證據品質 (20%)</td><td>6/10</td><td>AgentBench chart 有力，但 60-90% 無實測；TikTok 數字被延伸使用</td></tr><tr><td>D. 技術深度 (15%)</td><td>8/10</td><td>Context window growth analysis、cache preservation 機制、depth 5 hierarchy analogy 深度足夠</td></tr><tr><td>E. 泛化性 (15%)</td><td>8/10</td><td>適用所有多模型 agent 系統設計者；tokenomics 框架對任何組織使用 AI 的決策者有意義</td></tr><tr><td><strong>加權總分</strong></td><td><strong>7.30/10</strong></td><td>7×0.3+8×0.2+6×0.2+8×0.15+8×0.15</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>整合決策</strong>：Rule <strong>理由</strong>：核心是 agent 架構路由原則（frontier as manager / trailing-edge as workers / subagent as cache-efficient routing boundary），可直接入庫為 subagent-strategy.md 規則。</p>',35)])])}const m=t(r,[["render",s]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
